nodecode-mqtt is a rewrite of JasonSwindle's JasonSwindle/hermes-mqtt:
https://github.com/JasonSwindle/hermes-mqtt
commit 103ec28e9e2f68b080fd8e8074d0e5a98750e06e
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Jason Swindle (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the pair of operations the plugin was built on — publish
one event to a broker topic, and read what arrives on a topic — as the tools
mqtt_publish and mqtt_read, the Lisp verbs mqtt:publish and mqtt:read-topics
behind them, and a /mqtt command that reports the section and probes the
broker. Rewritten with them: the config surface (broker url, user, password,
both topic allowlists, timeout, payload cap), the gates the original kept (a
literal publish topic, deny-by-default allowlists that a read cannot widen, a
payload scanned for credential shapes, a size cap), and the thin JSON
envelope (schema, kind, timestamp, a per-process nonce, the data). The
protocol is spoken directly over the image's socket support — MQTT 3.1.1
CONNECT/CONNACK, SUBSCRIBE/SUBACK, PUBLISH/PUBACK at QoS 1, with TLS for an
mqtts:// url — so the rewrite carries no client library and no CLI.

What was left out: the platform adapter, and with it everything that needed
a resident gateway process: the one long-lived subscription, the per-topic
routing to a delivered chat line, an agent turn or a cron job, burst
coalescing, the reconnect supervisor, the de-duplication cache, the MQTT 5.0
packet surface (properties, reason codes, broker limits, the protocol ladder)
and the own-topic guard on replies. A cell is a verb in a session rather than
a process of its own: it opens a connection, does one thing and leaves, so a
watcher of a bus is a loop the operator runs against the same broker, and the
config is the section rather than a platform's extra map.
